Let the DRM switch actually fetch the CDM, for the one component that needs it.

Reported on Linux: a player says "failed to init media keys for EME", and a
test page reports only org.w3.clearkey with security level none. That is a
browser with no Widevine CDM at all, and it is what every Aerium desktop build
has been. The switch aerium-drm-media.patch added could never have done
anything.

Four facts, each read out of the 152 sources rather than recalled:

  enable_widevine=true comes from ungoogled-chromium's flags.gn, so the EME
  plumbing compiles and the key system looks like it is there.

  bundle_widevine_cdm is enable_library_widevine_cdm && (is_chrome_branded ||
  is_chrome_for_testing_branded) in third_party/widevine/cdm/widevine.gni. We
  are neither, so it is false and GetBundledWidevine() is not compiled at all.

  enable_widevine_cdm_component is computed, not a build argument, and is true
  for us - so GetHintedWidevine() IS compiled. It reads a hint file that only
  Component Updater writes.

  Component Updater cannot write it: component_updater_url_constants.cc is in
  ungoogled-chromium's domain_substitution.list, and the regex
  google([A-Za-z\-]*?\\*?)\.com rewrites update.googleapis.com to a host that
  does not resolve.

So AddWidevine() falls through to its own "Widevine enabled but no library
found" and registers nothing, leaving ClearKey - built into Chromium, needing
no CDM - as the only key system. Which is the report, exactly.

The component is already the right mechanism, so this lets it run rather than
replacing it. It fetches over the update protocol, verifies the CRX3 signature,
unpacks, and then writes the hint file on Linux or calls
CdmRegistry::RegisterCdm() directly everywhere else. Hand-rolling a downloader
would have meant hand-rolling signature verification for a binary that gets
dlopen'd, which is not a thing to hand-roll.

There is no simpler source to fetch from. The dl.google.com/widevine-cdm bucket
the old helper scripts use is gone: versions.txt and every version-specific zip
return 404 now.

Two files, both chosen because neither is in domain_substitution.list - checked,
not assumed - and apply_patches runs before apply_domsub in scripts/build.sh, so
a real hostname written in them survives the rewrite.

ChromeConfigurator::UpdateUrl() returns the real service while the switch is on.
It reads pref_service_, which browser_process_impl.cc sets to
g_browser_process->local_state() - the same PrefService aerium.drm_enabled is
registered in - and which the constructor CHECKs. No new global and no thread
question: the member is already there.

RegisterComponentsForUpdate() registers the Widevine CDM component and returns,
so it is the only component registered at all, and it watches the pref so that
flipping the switch takes effect in the running session.

WHY THE NARROWNESS IS REQUIRED, and why it costs nothing.

UpdateUrl() is a property of the configurator, not of a component: a real URL
is a real URL for everything registered against it. Turning DRM on must
therefore leave exactly one thing registered, or it would quietly hand Google
an update check for thirty-odd other components. At startup the gate does that
by returning early. Flipping the switch at runtime arrives too late for that -
everything was registered while DRM was off - so that path unregisters what is
there before registering the CDM, which puts the session in the state a launch
with DRM on would have been in.

Dropping the rest was audited rather than waved through. Every one of them has
spent its whole life pointed at the rewritten host, so none has ever downloaded
a payload, so none has an on-disk copy for its installer to load and no
ComponentReady() has ever fired for any of them in this browser. Unregistering
removes nothing that was doing anything. The ones worth naming because they
sound load-bearing - CRLSet, PKI metadata, file type policies, SSL error
assistant - are exactly in that position already: they are empty here today,
with or without this patch, and the fallbacks they use are compiled into the
binary rather than delivered by the component.

WHAT THE USER SEES. Turning it on now starts the download immediately. On
Windows and Mac that is the whole flow, because ComponentReady() registers the
CDM at runtime. On Linux the CDM must be registered before the zygote sandbox
closes, so it takes one restart after the download lands - which is Chromium's
design and not something a patch can remove. Before the pref watcher it took
two restarts, one to begin the download and one to use it.

Stated plainly, because the switch now means more than it did: turning DRM on
contacts Google's update service. It asks about one component and downloads one
thing, and the string under the switch says so.

Off by default, unchanged. PingUrl() is deliberately left rewritten - pings are
telemetry, a failed ping is non-fatal to the update flow, and fetching a CDM
does not need one.

VERIFIED, not assumed. The component's app id was derived from
kWidevineSha2Hash in widevine_cdm_component_installer.cc rather than recalled,
giving oimompecagnajdejgnnjijobebaeigek, and a real update request for it to the
URL written below returns HTTP 200, status "ok", Widevine CDM 4.10.3050.0, and
a 21,685,984-byte .crx3 with its SHA-256. Range-fetching that package returns
the Cr24 magic and format version 3, so the mirror serves a real signed CRX3.
Its hostnames arrive in the response body rather than from source text, so
domain substitution never touches them.

Both hunks were generated with git diff against the real 152.0.7977.82 files
and verified to apply with the same flags build.py uses.

The no_destructor include is base/no_destructor.h. It was written from memory
as base/memory/no_destructor.h in the first version of this patch, which is not
a path that exists, and pre-flight 33 stopped on it with "file not found" after
fifty-two minutes. Every added include here is now checked against the real
tree before the patch is committed.

--- a/chrome/browser/component_updater/chrome_component_updater_configurator.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/component_updater/chrome_component_updater_configurator.cc
@@ -161,6 +161,26 @@ base::TimeDelta ChromeConfigurator::UpdateDelay() const {
 
 std::vector<GURL> ChromeConfigurator::UpdateUrl() const {
   DCHECK_CALLED_ON_VALID_SEQUENCE(sequence_checker_);
+  // Aerium: the real update service, and only while the user has DRM switched
+  // on. This file is not in ungoogled-chromium's domain_substitution.list, and
+  // apply_patches runs before apply_domsub in scripts/build.sh, so the host
+  // written here survives - where the same string in
+  // component_updater_url_constants.cc does not. That rewrite is why the
+  // Widevine CDM component has never been able to download anything.
+  //
+  // pref_service_ is g_browser_process->local_state(), which is where
+  // aerium.drm_enabled is registered - browser_process_impl.cc passes it to
+  // MakeChromeComponentUpdaterConfigurator, and the constructor CHECKs it.
+  //
+  // This is a property of the configurator, not of a component, so it applies
+  // to everything registered. RegisterComponentsForUpdate() in registration.cc
+  // is what makes sure that is only ever the CDM.
+  //
+  // PingUrl() is deliberately left rewritten: pings are telemetry, a failed
+  // ping is non-fatal to the update flow, and fetching a CDM does not need one.
+  if (pref_service_->GetBoolean("aerium.drm_enabled")) {
+    return {GURL("https://update.googleapis.com/service/update2/json")};
+  }
   return configurator_impl_.UpdateUrl();
 }
 
